menuconfig TASK_WDT
    bool "Task watchdog"
    default y if WATCHDOG
    help
      Software watchdog that catches stuck threads, and the one feeding
      the hardware watchdog: without it, a hardware watchdog the SoC has
      started resets the system.

if TASK_WDT

config TASK_WDT_CHANNELS
    int "Channels"
    range 1 8
    default 8
    help
      Threads the task watchdog can watch at once.

config TASK_WDT_TIMEOUT_MS
    int "System thread timeout in milliseconds"
    default 5000
    help
      Timeout the system threads (KernelMain, SysWorkq, SysWorkqHigh)
      register their channels with.

config TASK_WDT_CHECK_PERIOD_MS
    int "Check period in milliseconds"
    default 3000 if WATCHDOG && WATCHDOG_TIMEOUT_MS < 20000
    default 10000
    help
      How often the watchdog thread checks its channels and feeds the
      hardware watchdog, waking the system up to do so. Must stay below
      half of the hardware watchdog timeout.

config TASK_WDT_GRACE_MS
    int "Grace period in milliseconds"
    default 1000
    help
      How long a channel may stay expired after a check first finds it
      expired, with its callback running on every check, before the
      system resets with a core dump. While a channel is expired, the
      checks run at most this far apart.

config TASK_WDT_THREAD_PRIO
    int "Watchdog thread priority"
    default 4

config TASK_WDT_THREAD_STACK_SIZE
    int "Watchdog thread stack size in bytes"
    default 1536
    help
      The expiry path logs synchronously and runs the channel callbacks
      on this stack; the QEMU build uses about 700 bytes of it there.

module = TASK_WDT
module-str = Task watchdog
source "subsys/logging/Kconfig.template.log_level"

endif
